# Unity's own meta/.meta files are tracked; generated Resources copies of the json are tracked too
# EXCEPT the IMPORT-NEUTRAL mirror (2026-11-27, BUG-058): unity/Assets/Art/Kit is a byte-for-byte copy of
# art/kit that nobody loads at runtime (the game reads Assets/Resources). Unity still writes a .meta next
# to every file in it the first time the project opens, and 140 untracked metas turn a clean install into a
# "working tree not clean (264 entries)" install -- which blocks Build-Bundle and made the player's
# Apply-Bundle report a dirty tree. The metas that MATTER (Resources: isReadable, point filter, PPU 16)
# stay tracked; these do not.
unity/Assets/Art.meta
unity/Assets/Art/**/*.meta
# scratch clones used by script tests
.idtest/
# agent scratch dirs (script tests) -- keep them out of the repo, always
.t*/
.scratch/
.tmpcheck/

# local toolchain (not shipped)
.tooling/

# ---------- local toolchain (2026-11-26) ----------
# The .NET SDK / PowerShell tarballs must NEVER live inside the workspace: 4,644 files blew the
# 10,000-file / 128 MB snapshot budget and silently dropped 4,283 files from the session. Install
# them under /tmp (or outside the repo) and leave .tooling with only its .keep marker.
.tooling/*
!.tooling/.keep
.dotnet/
.nuget/
tooling-history/

# ---------- Unity, generated on first open (unity/ is the project, not the repo root) ----------
# These are per-machine outputs of opening the project; ProjectSettings/ and Packages/ ARE committed.
unity/ProjectSettings/boot.config
unity/ProjectSettings/UnityConnectSettings.asset
unity/ProjectSettings/XRSettings.asset
unity/ProjectSettings/SceneTemplateSettings.json
unity/ProjectSettings/VirtualProjectsConfig.json
unity/ProjectSettings/Packages/
unity/ProjectSettings/*.pidb
unity/Packages/packages-lock.json
unity/*.csproj
unity/*.sln
unity/Assembly-CSharp*.csproj
unity/.vsconfig
unity/Assets/AssetStoreTools*

# ==== unity/Assets/Resources/Art/ is NOT ignored any more (2026-11-25) ====
# It used to be, on the reasoning that it is derived from art/kit/ every time. That reasoning was wrong
# for one specific reason: DeskSceneBuilder and DeskBootstrap load through Resources.Load, and
# Resources.Load cannot see anything outside a Resources folder. Unity imports from the project folder
# and never runs python, so a fresh clone with this folder ignored opens, Press Play, and logs
#   "sprite not found at Resources/Art/Kit/backgrounds/office_L0"
# while the art sits right there in art/kit/. A file the game READS AT RUNTIME is a source file.
# publish_to_unity.py writes this tree; the art gate verifies it byte-for-byte against art/kit.

